)}\n   \\]\n\n5. Recognize the decomposition \\(AD = AE + ED\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AD}{AE} = 1 + \\frac{ED}{AE}\n   \\]\n\n6. Substituting from step 4:\n   \\[\n   1 + \\frac{ED}{AE} = \\frac{AC(AB + AF)}{AF(AC + AB)}\n   \\]\n\n7. Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{ED}{AE} = \\frac{AC(AB + AF) - AF(AC + AB)}{AF(AC + AB)}\n   \\]\n\n8. To solve \\(\\frac{ED}{AE}\\), note the balance property of the incenter splits:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AB(AF)}{AF(AC + AB)} = \\frac{AC \\cdot (AB - AF)}{AF \\cdot (AC + AB)}\n   \\]\n\n9. By properties of the angle bisector, \\(BF\\) splits \\(\\angle B\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{AF}{CF} \\implies \\frac{AB}{AF} = \\frac{BC}{CF}\n   \\]\n\n10. Substituting in the equation:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{ED}{AE} = \\frac{AB}{AF} \\cdot \\frac{CF}{AC + AB}\n    \\]\n\n11. From this, we deduce:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{ED}{AE} = \\frac{b+c}{a}\n    \\]\n\n12. Concluding:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{AE}{ED} = \\frac{b+c}{a}\n    \\]\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\frac{b+c}{a}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(ABC\\) be an acute-angled triangle. Let \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) be points on \\(\\overline{BC}\\) and \\(\\overline{AC}\\), respectively, such that \\(\\overline{AD} \\perp \\overline{BC}\\) and \\(\\overline{BE} \\perp \\overline{AC}\\). Let \\(P\\) be the point where \\(\\overrightarrow{AD}\\) meets the semicircle constructed outwardly on \\(\\overline{BC}\\), and \\(Q\\) the point where \\(\\overrightarrow{BE}\\) meets the semicircle constructed outwardly on \\(\\overline{AC}\\). Prove that \\(PC = QC\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that PC equals QC in this acute-angled triangle ABC. Let me start by visualizing the problem. We have triangle ABC with altitudes AD and BE. D is on BC, E is on AC. Then, we extend AD and BE to meet semicircles constructed outwardly on BC and AC, respectively, at points P and Q. The goal is to show PC = QC.\n\nFirst, I should recall tha