ts of the progression:**\n    - The elements of the arithmetic progression are:\n      \\[\n      a_1 = 1, \\quad a_2 = p, \\quad a_3 = 2p - 1, \\quad a_4 = 3p - 2, \\quad \\ldots\n      \\]\n\n3. **Analyzing for different primes \\( p \\):**\n   - **Case \\( p = 2 \\):**\n     - If \\( p = 2 \\), then \\( r = 1 \\), and the sequence is \\( 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, n-1 \\).\n     - All numbers less than \\( n \\) are relatively prime to \\( n \\).\n     - This implies \\( n \\) is a prime number.\n\n   - **Case \\( p = 3 \\):**\n     - If \\( p = 3 \\), then \\( r = 2 \\), and the sequence is \\( 1, 3, 5, \\ldots \\).\n     - All odd numbers less than \\( n \\) are relatively prime to \\( n \\).\n     - Therefore, \\( n \\) has no odd divisors, implying that \\( n \\) is a power of 2, i.e., \\( n = 2^k \\) for some \\( k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\).\n\n   - **Case \\( p > 3 \\):**\n     - If \\( p > 3 \\), then \\( r = p - 1 \\), and we ascertain that \\( a_k = a_1 + (k-1)r = 1 + (k-1)(p-1) \\).\n     - Since \\( n - 1 \\) must be in the sequence, we have \\( p-1 \\mid n-2 \\).\n     - Let \\( q \\) be any prime divisor of \\( p-1 \\). Then \\( q \\mid n-2 \\) and also \\( q \\mid n \\).\n     - Since \\( q < p \\), \\( q \\mid n \\), this necessitates that \\( q = 2 \\).\n     - Thus, \\( p-1 \\) can only have 2 as a prime divisor, implying \\( p = 2^m + 1 \\) for some \\( m \\geq 2 \\).\n     - For \\( p \\) to be a prime and for \\( \\text{2p-1} \\) being relatively prime to \\( n \\): \\( \\text{2p-1} = 2^{m+1} + 1 \\) must not be divisible by 3.\n     - Note that for \\( 2^m + 1 \\) to remain prime, \\( m \\) must be even, else \\( 2^{m+1} + 1 \\mod 3 = 0 \\).\n\nBy conducting the above checks:\n\n- If the arithmetic progression exists as described, \\( n \\) must either be a prime number or a power of 2. \n\nThus, we conclude \\(\\boxed{n \\text{ is a prime number or a natural power of two}}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code"