c{B}{q}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the left-hand side (LHS) using \\( \\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   t^{1/p} B^{1/p + 1/q} = t^{1/p} B\n   \\]\n   The right-hand side (RHS) becomes:\n   \\[\n   B\\left(\\frac{t}{p} + \\frac{1}{q}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Dividing both sides by \\( B \\):\n   \\[\n   t^{1/p} \\leq \\frac{t}{p} + \\frac{1}{q}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Function Analysis:** Define \\( f(t) = t^{1/p} - \\frac{t}{p} - \\frac{1}{q} \\). Compute its derivatives:\n   - First derivative: \\( f'(t) = \\frac{1}{p} t^{(1/p)-1} - \\frac{1}{p} \\)\n   - Setting \\( f'(t) = 0 \\) yields \\( t = 1 \\).\n   - Second derivative: \\( f''(t) = \\frac{1}{p} \\left(\\frac{1}{p} - 1\\right) t^{(1/p)-2} \\). For \\( p > 1 \\), \\( f''(t) < 0 \\), indicating a maximum at \\( t = 1 \\).\n\n3. **Conclusion:** Since \\( f(1) = 0 \\) and \\( f(t) \\) has a maximum at \\( t = 1 \\), \\( f(t) \\leq 0 \\) for all \\( t > 0 \\). Thus:\n   \\[\n   A^{1/p} B^{1/q} \\leq \\frac{A}{p} + \\frac{B}{q}\n   \\]\n   Equality holds if \\( A = B \\).\n\n**Case 2: \\( p < 1 \\)**\n\n1. **Substitution:** Similarly, let \\( t = \\frac{A}{B} \\). The inequality transforms to:\n   \\[\n   t^{1/p} \\geq \\frac{t}{p} + \\frac{1}{q}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Function Analysis:** Using the same \\( f(t) \\), compute derivatives:\n   - For \\( p < 1 \\), \\( f''(t) > 0 \\) (whether \\( p \\) is positive or negative), indicating a minimum at \\( t = 1 \\).\n\n3. **Conclusion:** Since \\( f(1) = 0 \\) and \\( f(t) \\) has a minimum at \\( t = 1 \\), \\( f(t) \\geq 0 \\) for all \\( t > 0 \\). Thus:\n   \\[\n   A^{1/p} B^{1/q} \\geq \\frac{A}{p} + \\frac{B}{q}\n   \\]\n   Equality holds if \\( A = B \\).\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n- For \\( p > 1 \\), \\( \\boxed{A^{1/p} B^{1/q} \\leq \\frac{A}{p} + \\frac{B}{q}} \\).\n- For \\( p < 1 \\), \\( \\boxed{A^{1/p} B^{1/q} \\geq \\frac{A}{p} + \\frac{B}{q}} \\).\n\nBoth inequalities are strict when \\( A \\neq B \\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the conditions:\n- \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are positive numbers,\n- \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are rational numbers such that \\( \\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = 1 \\).\n\nWe are asked to prove two inequalities based on whether \\(p > 1\\) or \\(p < 1\\).\n\nLet us proceed step-by-step:\n\n1. **Substitution and Reformulation**:\n   Define \\(x = \\frac{A}{B}\\), \\(a = \\frac{1}{p}\\), and \\(1 - a = \\frac{1}{q}\\).\n\n   By these definitions, our inequality becomes:\n   \\[\n   B \\left( x^a - a x + a - 1 \\right) = A^{1/p} B^{1/q} - \\frac{A}{p} - \\frac{B}{q}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Utilization of Results from Problem 8.44**:\n   To utilize this, let us examine the function \\( f(x) = x^a - a x + a - 1 \\):\n   - If \\( p > 1 \\): \\( 0 < a < 1 \\).\n   - If \\( p < 1 \\): \\( a > 1 \\).\n\n3. **Analyzing the Function**:\n   Let's analyze the function \\( f(x) = x^a - a x + a - 1 \\) over the relevant domain:\n   - For \\( p > 1 \\): \\( 0 < a < 1 \\).\n     We want to show that \\( f(x) \\leq 0 \\). If \\( A \\neq B \\), the function should be strictly less than 0. This inequality utilizes properties of convexity and the behavior of exponential functions.\n   \n   - For \\( p < 1 \\): \\( a > 1 \\).\n     We want to show that \\( f(x) \\geq 0 \\). Similarly, if \\( A \\neq B \\), it should be strictly greater than 0.\n\n4. **Convexity and Derivatives**:\n   Through calculus, specifically differentiation, we can confirm the behavior of the function for the given ranges of \\(a\\). These can be related back to problem 8.44 which discusses such exponential inequalities.\n\n### Conclusion:\n- If \\( p > 1 \\):\n  \\[\n  A^{1/p} B^{1/q} \\leq \\frac{A}{p} + \\frac{B}{q}.\n  \\]\n- If \\( p < 1 \\):\n  \\[\n  A^{1/p} B^{1/q} \\geq \\frac{A}{p} + \\frac{B}{q}.\n  \\]\n\nIf \\( A \\neq B \\), the inequalities are strict.\n\nTherefore, the finalized boxed inequalities are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \n\\begin{cases} \nA^{1/p} B^{1/q} \\leq \\frac{A}{p} + \\frac{B}{q}, & \\text{if } p > 1, \\\\\nA^{1/p} B^{1/q} \\geq \\frac{A}{p} + \\frac{B}{q}, & \\text{if } p < 1.\n\\end{cases}\n}\n\\]\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Pigsy picked a bag of wild fruits from the mountain to bring back to Tang Monk. As he walked, he got hungry and took out half of them, hesitated, put back two, and then ate the rest. This happened four times: taking out half, putting back two, and then eating. By the time he reached Tang Monk, there were 5 wild fruits left in the bag. How many wild fruits did Pigsy originally pick?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. Pigsy picked a bag of wild fruits and then every time he got hungry, he took out half of them, put back two, and then ate the rest. This happened four times, and in the end, there were 5 fruits left. We need to find out how many he originally picked.\n\nHmm. So each time he does the same process: take out half, put back two, eat the rest. Wait, but the problem says \"took out half of them, hesitated, put back two, and then ate the rest.\" So maybe the process is: he takes out half of the fruits from the bag, then he hesitates and puts back two into the bag, and then he eats the rest that he took out? Or does he take out half, then put back two into the bag, and then eat the remaining ones he took out?\n\nWait, maybe the sequence is: He takes out half of the fruits from the bag. Then, he puts back two into the bag. Then, he eats the rest that he had taken out. So the total number of fruits in the bag after each time would be the original half minus two? Wait, no. Let's parse it again.\n\nOriginal action: \"took out half of them, hesitated, put back two, and then ate the rest.\" So \"took out half of them\" \u2013 so he takes half of the current fruits in the bag out. Then he hesitates, so he puts back two fruits into the bag. Then he eats the rest of the ones he had taken out. So the net effect on the bag is that he took out half, then put back two. But how much does he eat?\n\nWait, let's think step by step for one iteration. Let's say the bag has N fruits. He takes out half of them, which is N/2. Then he puts back two into the bag. So the bag now has N/2 (the remaining half) plus the two he put back, making it N/2 + 2. But wait, that can't be right because he took out half, so the bag has N/2 left. Then he puts back two, so the bag has N/2 + 2. But then he eats the rest of the ones he took out. He took out N/2, put back two into the bag, so the amount he ate would be N/2 - 2. Because he put back two into the bag from the half he took out, so he ate the remaining N/2 - 2.\n\nSo each time, the number of fruits in the bag after the process is N/2 + 2, and he eats N/2 - 2.\n\nBut the problem says this happened four times, and after four times, there were 5 fruits left. So we need to reverse-engineer the original number.\n\nThis is a typical problem that can be solved by working backwards. Let's try that.\n\nStarting from the end: after the fourth time, there are 5 fruits left. Let's figure out how many were there before the fourth time.\n\nLet\u2019s denote the number of fruits before each eating event as x_i, where i is the step. So after the fourth time, x_4 = 5. We need to find x_3, the number of fruits before the fourth eating event.\n\nFrom the process: after taking out half, putting back two, and eating the rest, the remaining fruits are x_i+1 = (x_i / 2) + 2. Wait, but if he took out half (x_i / 2), put back two, so the bag now has (x_i / 2) - (x_i / 2) + 2? Wait, no. Wait, if he takes out half of the fruits, the bag has half left. Then he puts back two into the bag. So the bag now has (x_i / 2) + 2. Then he eats the rest of the taken-out fruits, which were x_i / 2, but he put back two, so he ate (x_i / 2) - 2. So the fruits remaining in the bag after this process is (x_i / 2) + 2.\n\nTherefore, the next state is (x_i / 2) + 2. So we can model this as x_{i+1} = (x_i / 2) + 2. But we need to reverse this process. Starting from x_4 = 5, we need to find x_3 such that x_4 = (x_3 / 2) + 2. Solving for x_3: x_3 = (x_4 - 2) * 2. Similarly, this applies for each step.\n\nSo working backwards:\n\nAfter fourth time: 5 fruits.\n\nBefore fourth time: Let\u2019s call the number before the fourth time x_3. Then x_4 = (x_3 / 2) + 2 => x_3 = (5 - 2) * 2 = 3 * 2 = 6.\n\nThird time: The number before third time is x_2. Then x_3 = (x_2 / 2) + 2 => x_2 = (6 - 2) * 2 = 4 * 2 = 8.\n\nSecond time: x_1 = (8 - 2) * 2 = 6 * 2 = 12.\n\nFirst time: Original number x_0 = (12 - 2) * 2 = 10 * 2 = 20.\n\nWait, so if we work backwards like that, the original number would be 20. Let's check this.\n\nStarting with 20:\n\nFirst time: takes out half, which is 10. Puts back 2, so the bag now has 10 (remaining half) + 2 = 12. He eats 10 - 2 = 8.\n\nSecond time: bag has 12. Takes out half, 6. Puts back 2, bag becomes 6 + 2 = 8. He eats 6 - 2 = 4.\n\nThird time: bag has 8. Takes out half, 4. Puts back 2, bag becomes 4 + 2 = 6. He eats 4 - 2 = 2.\n\nFourth time: bag has 6. Takes out half, 3. Puts back 2, bag becomes 3 + 2 = 5. He eats 3 - 2 = 1.\n\nYes! After four times, the bag has 5 left. So original number is 20.\n\nBut wait, the problem says \"he took out half of them, hesitated, put back two, and then ate the rest.\" So when he takes out half, he puts back two into the bag, and eats the rest of the taken-out half. So the eaten amount is (half taken out) minus 2. But when we work backwards, we model the remaining in the bag as (x_i / 2) + 2, which gives us the next state. Then, reversing it by (x_{i+1} - 2) * 2 gives the previous state. Which works here.\n\nSo the answer should be 20. Let me check again.\n\nStarting with 20:\n\nFirst iteration:\n\nTake out half: 10. Put back 2, so bag has 10 (remaining) + 2 = 12. He eats 8.\n\nSecond iteration:\n\nTake out half of 12: 6. Put back 2, bag has 6 + 2 = 8. Eats 4.\n\nThird iteration:\n\nTake out half of 8: 4. Put back 2, bag has 4 + 2 = 6. Eats 2.\n\nFourth iteration:\n\nTake out half of 6: 3. Put back 2, bag has 3 + 2 = 5. Eats 1.\n\nYes, after four times, 5 left. So original was 20. That seems correct.\n\nBut let me think again, sometimes in these problems, people might confuse the operations. Let's verify once more.\n\nEach time, the operations are:\n\n1. Take out half of the current amount in the bag.\n\n2. Put back two into the bag.\n\n3. Eat the remaining taken-out fruits.\n\nSo the fruits remaining in the bag after each operation are:\n\nOriginal amount: N\n\nAfter taking out half: N/2 left in the bag, N/2 taken out.\n\nPut back two: so add 2 to the bag, so N/2 + 2 in the bag.\n\nThe amount eaten is (N/2 taken out) - 2 (since he put back two into the bag). So he eats (N/2 - 2).\n\nTherefore, each operation reduces the total fruits in the bag according to N \u2192 N/2 + 2.\n\nSo working backwards, if after the operation, the number is x_{i+1}, then before the operation it was x_i = 2*(x_{i+1} - 2).\n\nSo starting from 5:\n\nFourth time: x_4 = 5.\n\nThird time before eating: x_3 = 2*(5 - 2) = 6.\n\nSecond time before: x_2 = 2*(6 - 2) = 8.\n\nFirst time before: x_1 = 2*(8 - 2) = 12.\n\nOriginal: x_0 = 2*(12 - 2) = 20.\n\nYes, same result. Therefore, the original number is 20.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of wild fruits Pigsy originally picked is \\boxed{20}.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the original number of wild fruits Pigsy picked, we work backwards from the final amount of 5 fruits left after four iterations of taking out half, putting back two, and eating the rest. \n\n1. **Fourth Iteration:**\n   - Fruits left: 5\n   - Let \\( x_3 \\) be the number of fruits before the fourth iteration.\n   - Equation: \\( 5 = \\frac{x_3}{2} + 2 \\)\n   - Solving: \\( x_3 = (5 - 2) \\times 2 = 3 \\times 2 = 6 \\)\n\n2. **Third Iteration:**\n   - Fruits left: 6\n   - Let \\( x_2 \\) be the number of fruits before the third iteration.\n   - Equation: \\( 6 = \\frac{x_2}{2} + 2 \\)\n   - Solving: \\( x_2 = (6 - 2) \\times 2 = 4 \\times 2 = 8 \\)\n\n3. **Second Iteration:**\n   - Fruits left: 8\n   - Let \\( x_1 \\) be the number of fruits before the second iteration.\n   - Equation: \\( 8 = \\frac{x_1}{2} + 2 \\)\n   - Solving: \\( x_1 = (8 - 2) \\times 2 = 6 \\times 2 = 12 \\)\n\n4. **First Iteration:**\n   - Fruits left: 12\n   - Let \\( x_0 \\) be the original number of fruits before the first iteration.\n   - Equation: \\( 12 = \\frac{x_0}{2} + 2 \\)\n   - Solving: \\( x_0 = (12 - 2) \\times 2 = 10 \\times 2 = 20 \\)\n\nThus, the original number of wild fruits Pigsy picked is \\(\\boxed{20}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem\nPigsy (\u732a\u516b\u6212) picked a bag of wild fruits to take back to Tang Monk (\u5510\u50e7). While walking, he got hungry and took out half of the fruits from the bag. Just as he was about to eat, he hesitated, put back two fruits, and then ate the rest. He repeated this process a total of four times. When he finally returned to Tang Monk, there were still 5 fruits left in the bag. How many fruits did Pigsy originally pick?\n\n\nTo find out how many fruits Pigsy picked originally, let\u2019s denote the initial number of fruits by \\( x \\). We\u2019ll work backwards from the final state (5 fruits) to determine the initial amount. We will reverse each step Pigsy took:\n\n1. When he returned, he had 5 fruits. Let \\[ y_4 = 5 \\]\n\n2. Before the final step (fourth time he ate the fruits): \n   - He ate the remaining fruits after putting back two, meaning before adding these two fruits back, he ate \\( y_4 - 2 \\).\n   - Since he ate half of what was in the bag at that point, before taking out the half, there must have been twice the amount of this minus the 2 fruits added back:\n     \\[\n     y_3 = 2(y_4 - 2) = 2(5 - 2) = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n     \\]\n\n3. Third time, before adding two fruits back: \n   \\[\n   y_2 = 2(y_3 - 2) = 2(6 - 2) = 2 \\times 4 = 8\n   \\]\n\n4. Second time, before adding two fruits back:\n   \\[\n   y_1 = 2(y_2 - 2) = 2(8 - 2) = 2 \\times 6 = 12\n   \\]\n\n5. First time, before adding two fruits back:\n   \\[\n   x = 2(y_1 - 2) = 2(12 - 2) = 2 \\times 10 = 20\n   \\]\n\nThus, Pigsy originally picked 20 wild fruits. Therefore, the initial number of wild fruits he picked is:\n\\[ \n\\boxed{20}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Among all the simple fractions where both the numerator and the denominator are two-digit numbers, find the smallest fraction that is greater than $\\frac{3}{5}$. Provide the numerator of this fraction in your answer.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the smallest fraction greater than 3/5 where both the numerator and the denominator are two-digit numbers. Hmm, let me think through this step by step. First, I know that 3/5 is equal to 0.6, right? So I'm looking for the smallest fraction that's just a bit bigger than 0.6, but both the top and bottom numbers have to be two-digit numbers. \n\nLet me start by recalling that if I have a fraction a/b, I want a/b > 3/5. Which means a/b > 0.6. To find the smallest such fraction, it's like finding the minimum a/b that is still above 0.6. Since both a and b are two-digit numbers, they range from 10 to 99. \n\nMaybe I can approach this by fixing the denominator and finding the smallest numerator that makes the fraction exceed 0.6. For each denominator b (from 10 to 99), the smallest numerator a would be the smallest integer greater than 0.6*b. Then, check if that a is a two-digit number. If so, compute a/b and keep track of the minimum such fraction. \n\nWait, but doing this for all denominators from 10 to 99 might be time-consuming, but maybe there's a pattern or a way to narrow it down. Let me try to formalize this. For each two-digit denominator b, the minimal a is the ceiling of 0.6*b. Then, the fraction would be ceiling(0.6*b)/b. Then among all these fractions, I need the smallest one. \n\nAlternatively, since fractions can be compared by cross-multiplication, maybe there's a smarter way. Let me think. For two fractions a/b and c/d, a/b > c/d if ad > bc. So, if I want to compare two fractions a/b and the minimal one, maybe I can iterate through possible candidates. \n\nBut perhaps starting with denominators close to where 3/5 would sit. Let's see, 3/5 is 0.6. If the denominator is, say, 10, then the numerator would need to be 6.1 to get 0.61, but since a has to be two-digit, the smallest a would be 61? Wait, no. Wait, denominator is 10, but numerator is also two-digit. Wait, denominator must be two-digit. So denominator is from 10 to 99. Similarly, numerator is from 10 to 99. So, actually, the fractions are all between 10/99 (approx 0.101) and 99/10 (9.9). But we need the smallest fraction greater than 0.6 in this set. \n\nSo maybe the fractions just above 0.6 would have a numerator and denominator that are close in value. Let me think. Let's take the difference between numerator and denominator. If a/b > 3/5, then 5a > 3b, so 5a - 3b > 0. Maybe we can look for minimal 5a - 3b where 5a - 3b > 0 and a, b are two-digit numbers. Then the fraction a/b would be the minimal such that a/b > 3/5. \n\nBut how do we find the minimal a/b in this case? Because even if 5a - 3b is minimal positive, that doesn't necessarily mean a/b is minimal. For example, if 5a -3b =1, then a/b is just slightly over 3/5. So perhaps we need to find fractions where 5a - 3b is minimized but positive. Then the corresponding a/b would be the minimal fraction above 3/5. \n\nAlternatively, since a/b > 3/5, we can write this as a > (3/5)b. So for each denominator b, the smallest numerator a is the smallest integer greater than (3/5)b. So that would be floor((3/5)b) +1. Then compute a/b for each b, and find the minimal a/b. \n\nThis seems more systematic. Let me test this with some examples. Let's take denominator b = 10. Then (3/5)*10 = 6. So the smallest a is 7. But 7 is a single-digit number, so since a must be two-digit, the smallest a is 10. So 10/10 = 1, w