\nWait, let's clarify. Suppose point M is on the ray starting at A, making 70 degrees above AB. Then, angle MAB is 70 degrees. If M is on the ray starting at A, making 70 degrees below AB (i.e., in the opposite direction), then angle MAB is also 70 degrees. However, if M is behind A, meaning on the extension of AB beyond A, then angle MAB would be 180 degrees minus 70 degrees, which is 110 degrees. Therefore, those points would not satisfy the condition.\n\nTherefore, the locus is only the two rays starting at A and making 70-degree angles with AB, but not including the opposite sides. So, if AB is from A to B, then the two rays are in the direction away from B, forming 70 degrees on either side. Wait, but if the rays are starting at A, they can go infinitely in any direction. However, if M is on the side towards B, then the angle is 70 degrees. If it's on the other side, away from B, would the angle still be 70 degrees?\n\nWait, no. Let me consider specific coordinates again. Let\u2019s place A at (0,0), B at (1,0). If M is at some point (x,y). The angle MAB is the angle between vectors AB and AM. Vector AB is (1,0), and vector AM is (x,y). The angle between AB and AM can be found using the dot product formula:\n\ncos(theta) = (AB . AM) / (|AB||AM|)\n\nSo, theta = 70 degrees. Therefore,\n\n( (1)(x) + (0)(y) ) / (1 * sqrt(x^2 + y^2)) ) = cos(70\u00b0)\n\nTherefore,\n\nx / sqrt(x^2 + y^2) = cos(70\u00b0)\n\nSquaring both sides:\n\nx\u00b2 / (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2) = cos\u00b2(70\u00b0)\n\nMultiply both sides by (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2):\n\nx\u00b2 = cos\u00b2(70\u00b0)(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2)\n\nx\u00b2 = cos\u00b2(70\u00b0)x\u00b2 + cos\u00b2(70\u00b0)y\u00b2\n\nBring terms together:\n\nx\u00b2 - cos\u00b2(70\u00b0)x\u00b2 = cos\u00b2(70\u00b0)y\u00b2\n\nx\u00b2(1 - cos\u00b2(70\u00b0)) = cos\u00b2(70\u00b0)y\u00b2\n\nBut 1 - cos\u00b2(theta) = sin\u00b2(theta), so:\n\nx\u00b2 sin\u00b2(70\u00b0) = cos\u00b2(70\u00b0) y\u00b2\n\nDivide both sides by cos\u00b2(70\u00b0):\n\nx\u00b