uation are:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1}{2}, \\quad x = \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{13}}{4}, \\quad x = \\frac{-1 - \\sqrt{13}}{4}\n\\]\n\nThese roots satisfy the original ninth-degree equation, and checking the system confirms there are no additional real solutions. Therefore, the real solutions are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}, \\quad \\boxed{\\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{13}}{4}}, \\quad \\boxed{\\frac{-1 - \\sqrt{13}}{4}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe start with the given equation:\n\n\\[ x^{9} + \\frac{9}{8} x^{6} + \\frac{27}{64} x^{3} - x + \\frac{219}{512} = 0. \\]\n\n1. **Recognize Structure and Simplify:**\n    Notice that each term seems to have a pattern, lending the equation possible simplification through substitution. Let's assume:\n\\[ t = x^{3} \\]\nthen the equation becomes:\n\\[ t^3 + \\frac{9}{8} t^2 + \\frac{27}{64} t - \\sqrt[3]{t} + \\frac{219}{512} = 0. \\]\n    However, this substitution does not simplify easily. We will consider another simplification approach.\n\n2. **Alternate Simplification Approach:**\n    Let's rewrite the equation in a factorizable form by assuming a transformation:\n\\[ x^{9} + \\frac{9}{8} x^{6} + \\frac{27}{64} x^{3} - x + \\frac{219}{512} = 0 \\]\nWe aim to express the polynomial as a product of simpler polynomials.\n\n3. **Recognize the Polynomial Pattern:**\nExamining if this polynomial has any known root structures, notice:\n\\[ (x - a) (ax^2 + bx + c) = 0 \\]\nIf we postulate:\n\\[ x^{3} - x + \\frac{3}{8} = 0 \\]\n\n4. **Rewrite the Polynomial:**\nNow recall a simpler cubic form equivalent:\n\\[ \\sqrt[3]{x-\\frac{3}{8}} = x^{3} + \\frac{3}{8} \\]\n\n5. **Inverse Function Approach:**\nDefine \\( f(x) = x^3 + \\frac{3}{8} \\) and note both sides are monotonic and increasing.\n\n\\[ f(x) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad f^{-1}(x) \\]\nIf \\( f(x) = y = f^{-1}(x) \\), then \\( f(y) = x \\).\n\n6. **Equality and Simplification to Simpler Roots**:\nThis simplifies the problem to solving:\n\\[ x^3 - x + \\frac{3}{8} = 0 \\]\nUse synthetic