u03b8 = \u03b1/2 = 25\u00b0. Let's test \u03b8 = 25\u00b0, which would make \u03c6 = \u03b8 + 12.5\u00b0 = 37.5\u00b0.\n\nCompute LHS:\n\n1 + [ cos(25\u00b0 + 12.5\u00b0 ) / cos12.5\u00b0 ] = 1 + [ cos37.5\u00b0 / cos12.5\u00b0 ]\n\ncos37.5\u00b0 \u2248 0.7939, cos12.5\u00b0 \u2248 0.9763, so 0.7939 / 0.9763 \u2248 0.813. Therefore, LHS \u2248 1 + 0.813 = 1.813\n\nRHS: [ 2 sin25\u00b0 cos25\u00b0 ] / sin25\u00b0 = 2 cos25\u00b0 \u2248 2 * 0.9063 \u2248 1.8126\n\nSo, LHS \u2248 1.813, RHS \u2248 1.8126, which are approximately equal. So this suggests that \u03b8 = 25\u00b0, which would make \u03b1 = 50\u00b0, \u03b3 = 105\u00b0, is a solution.\n\nTherefore, angle ACB is 105\u00b0, so the answer is 105 degrees.\n\nLet me verify this solution.\n\nIf angle C is 105\u00b0, angle A is 50\u00b0, angle B is 25\u00b0, sum to 180\u00b0. Check.\n\nGiven that AC + AO = BC. Let's check if this holds.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the lengths. Let's assign a value to side AC = b. Let's set b = 1 for simplicity.\n\nThen, using the Law of Sines:\n\na / sin50\u00b0 = b / sin25\u00b0 \u21d2 a = (sin50\u00b0 / sin25\u00b0 ) * b \u2248 (0.7660 / 0.4226) * 1 \u2248 1.812\n\nSimilarly, c = AB = (sin105\u00b0 / sin25\u00b0 ) * b \u2248 (0.9659 / 0.4226) \u2248 2.287\n\nSemiperimeter s = (a + b + c)/2 \u2248 (1.812 + 1 + 2.287)/2 \u2248 (5.099)/2 \u2248 2.5495\n\nArea = (1/2)*a*c*sin25\u00b0 \u2248 0.5 * 1.812 * 2.287 * 0.4226 \u2248 0.5 * 1.812 * 2.287 * 0.4226 \u2248 0.5 * 1.812 * 0.967 \u2248 0.5 * 1.752 \u2248 0.876\n\nInradius r = Area / s \u2248 0.876 / 2.5495 \u2248 0.3435\n\nAO is the distance from A to the incenter. AO = r / sin(\u03b1/2 ) = 0.3435 / sin25\u00b0 \u2248 0.3435 / 0.4226 \u2248 0.812\n\nTherefore, AC + AO \u2248 1 + 0.812 \u2248 1.812, which is equal to BC \u2248 1.812. So the condition AC + AO = BC is satisfied.\n\nTherefore, angle C is indeed 105\u00b0, so the answer is 105 degrees.\n\nBut let me check another possible angle to ensure there are no other solutions. S