 Are there more?\n\nWait, maybe I missed some solutions. Let me check with another approach.\n\nStarting again, from the original equation:\n\nxy + x = y + 92.\n\nLet me rearrange terms:\n\nxy - y + x - 92 = 0.\n\nFactor y from the first two terms:\n\ny(x -1) + (x -92) = 0.\n\nSo, y(x -1) = 92 - x.\n\nTherefore, y = (92 - x)/(x -1). So as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can write the equation as:\n\nxy + x - y = 92.\n\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\nxy + x - y +1 = 93.\n\nThen, factor:\n\nx(y +1) - (y -1) =93.\n\nHmm, not sure. Alternatively, maybe factor:\n\nxy - y + x +1 =93 +1? Wait, that might not help.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider adding 1 to both sides:\n\nxy + x - y +1 =93.\n\nThen, the left side can be written as (x -1)(y +1).\n\nLet me check:\n\n(x -1)(y +1) = x(y +1) -1(y +1) = xy +x - y -1. But we have xy +x - y +1, which is 2 more. Hmm, not quite. Wait:\n\nWait, if we have (x -1)(y +1) = xy +x - y -1.\n\nBut in our equation, after adding 1, we have xy +x - y +1 =93. So:\n\n(x -1)(y +1) = 93 - 2 =91. Wait, no:\n\nWait, (x -1)(y +1) = xy +x - y -1. But we have xy +x - y +1 =93. So:\n\n(x -1)(y +1) = 93 -2=91. Wait, let's check:\n\nFrom xy +x - y +1 =93.\n\nWe can write xy +x - y -1 +2 =93 => (x -1)(y +1) +2=93 => (x -1)(y +1)=91.\n\nAh, there we go. So:\n\n(x -1)(y +1) =91.\n\nNow, since x and y are positive integers, x -1 and y +1 are positive integers as well. Because x >=1 (but x must be positive integer, so x >=1, but if x=1, then x -1=0, which would require 0*(y +1)=91, which is impossible. So x must be at least 2.\n\nTherefore, x -1 >=1 and y +1 >=2.\n\nSo, (x -1) and (y +1) are positive integer divisors of 91. Since 91=7*13=1*91.\n\nTherefore, the positive divisors of 91 are 1,7,13,91.\n\nTherefore, the possible pairs (x -1, y +1) are the factor pairs of 91:\n\n(1,91), (7,13), (13,7), (91,1).\n\nTherefore, for each factor pair (a,b) where a*b=91, we have:\n\nx -1 =a, so x =a +1\n\ny +1 =b, so y =b -1.\n\nTherefore, the possible solutions:\n\n1. (a=1