ing to our calculation, E|X|^p = [1/(2 C_p)] \u222b (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/|t|^{1 + p} dt, where C_p = \u222b_{0}^{infty} (1 - cos u)/u^{1 + p} du. Therefore, the constant in the problem is C_p = [\u222b_{R} (1 - cos t)/|t|^{1 + p} dt]^{-1} = [2 \u222b_{0}^{infty} (1 - cos t)/t^{1 + p} dt]^{-1} = [2 C_p]^{-1}, so then according to our expression, E|X|^p = [1/(2 C_p)] \u222b ... dt = C_p^{-1} * [1/(2 C_p)] \u222b ... dt. Wait, perhaps I need to re-express.\n\nWait, in the problem, C_p is defined as [\u222b_{R} (1 - cos t)/|t|^{1 + p} dt]^{-1}. Let me denote this integral as I_p = \u222b_{R} (1 - cos t)/|t|^{1 + p} dt = 2 \u222b_{0}^{infty} (1 - cos t)/t^{1 + p} dt. Then, C_p = I_p^{-1}. From our previous calculation, E|X|^p = [1/(2 C_p')] \u222b (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/|t|^{1 + p} dt, where C_p' = \u222b_{0}^{infty} (1 - cos u)/u^{1 + p} du = I_p / 2. Therefore, [1/(2 C_p')] = [1/(2 * (I_p / 2))] = [1/I_p] = C_p. Therefore, E|X|^p = C_p \u222b (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/|t|^{1 + p} dt. So that matches the problem statement. Therefore, this shows the equality when E|X|^p is finite. But we also need to show that E|X|^p < \u221e if and only if the integral \u222b (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/|t|^{1 + p} dt is finite.\n\nSo, the key step is that |X|^p is proportional to the integral of (1 - cos(tX))/|t|^{1 + p} dt. Therefore, if E|X|^p is finite, then the expectation of the integral is the integral of the expectation, which is finite. Conversely, if the integral of (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/|t|^{1 + p} dt is finite, then by the same identity, E|X|^p must be finite. Therefore, this establishes the equivalence.\n\nTherefore, part (a) can be proven by using this integral representation of |X|^p and then taking expectations, interchanging integral and expectation via Fubini's theorem, which is valid because the integrand is non-negative. Thus, the equivalence holds.\n\nNow, moving to part (b). It states that if E|X| < \u221e, then E|X| is equal to the negative of the improper Riemann integral of Re \u03c6'(t)/(\u03c0 t) dt over R. Here, \u03c6'(t) is the derivative of the characteristic function. Since \u03c6(t) = E[e^{itX}], then \u03c6'(t) = E[iX e^{itX}]. Therefore, Re \u03c6'(t) = Re E[iX e^{itX}] = E[ Re(iX e^{itX}) ] = E[ -X sin(tX) ], since Re(iX e^{itX}) = Re( iX (cos(tX) + i sin(tX)) ) = Re( -X sin(tX) + i X cos(tX) ) = -X sin(tX). Therefore, Re \u03c6'(t) = - E[X sin(tX)]. Hence, the integral in part (b) becomes - \u222b_{R} [ E[X sin(tX) ] / (\u03c0 t) ] dt. Then, E|X| is equal to the negative of this integral, so:\n\nE|X| = \u222b_{R} [ E[X sin(tX) ] / (\u03c0 t) ] dt.\n\nBut X sin(tX) is an odd function in X if t is fixed, but since we are integrating over t, perhaps there's a way to relate this to E|X|.\n\nAlternatively, note that |X| can be expressed as (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} (1 - cos(tX))/t^2 dt. But I need to verify this. Wait, for X \u2260 0, \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} (1 - cos(tX))/t^2 dt = |X| \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} (1 - cos u)/u^2 du, where u = t|X|. The integral \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} (1 - cos u)/u^2 du = \u03c0/2. Hence, \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} (1 - cos(tX))/t^2 dt = (\u03c0/2) |X|. Therefore, |X| = (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} (1 - cos(tX))/t^2 dt. Taking expectation, E|X| = (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} E[(1 - cos(tX))]/t^2 dt = (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 dt. However, the problem in part (b) is expressing E|X| in terms of the integral involving Re \u03c6'(t)/t. So perhaps we can relate (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 to Re \u03c6'(t)/t via integration by parts.\n\nLet me consider the integral \u222b_{R} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/(\u03c0 t^2) dt. But the expression in part (b) is \u222b Re \u03c6'(t)/(\u03c0 t) dt. Let's see. If we integrate by parts, taking u = (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/\u03c0 and dv = dt/t^2. Then, du = - Re \u03c6'(t)/\u03c0 dt and v = -1/t. Integration by parts gives:\n\n\u222b (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/(\u03c0 t^2) dt = [ - (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/(\u03c0 t) ]_{-\u221e}^{\u221e} + \u222b Re \u03c6'(t)/(\u03c0 t) dt.\n\nAssuming that the boundary terms vanish, which would require that (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t tends to 0 as |t| \u2192 \u221e. Since \u03c6(t) tends to 0 as |t| \u2192 \u221e (by Riemann-Lebesgue lemma, if X has a absolutely continuous distribution, but in general, \u03c6(t) may not tend to 0; but if E|X| < \u221e, then \u03c6 is differentiable and \u03c6'(t) is continuous, but I'm not sure if \u03c6(t) tends to 0. Wait, actually, the Riemann-Lebesgue lemma says that if X has an absolutely integrable characteristic function, which would be the case if it has a density, but in general, \u03c6(t) \u2192 0 as |t| \u2192 \u221e only if the distribution is absolutely continuous. However, if X has a finite first moment, then \u03c6 is differentiable and \u03c6' is continuous, but \u03c6(t) itself may not tend to 0. For example, if X is a lattice distribution, \u03c6(t) is periodic and does not tend to 0. Therefore, the boundary terms might not vanish. Hmm, this complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the integral in part (b) is an improper Riemann integral, which is conditionally convergent. The problem specifies (R) \u222b, which is the improper Riemann integral. So maybe even if the boundary terms don't vanish in the Lebesgue sense, they do in the Riemann sense.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the integral \u222b Re \u03c6'(t)/(\u03c0 t) dt is interpreted as the Cauchy principal value, i.e., lim_{A \u2192 \u221e} \u222b_{-A}^{A} Re \u03c6'(t)/(\u03c0 t) dt.\n\nBut let's go back to the expression for E|X|. From the earlier approach, E|X| = (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 dt. Let me split this integral into two parts: from 0 to \u03b5 and from \u03b5 to \u221e. For the integral from \u03b5 to \u221e, we can consider integration by parts:\n\n\u222b_{\u03b5}^{\u221e} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 dt = [ - (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t ]_{\u03b5}^{\u221e} + \u222b_{\u03b5}^{\u221e} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt.\n\nAssuming that as t \u2192 \u221e, (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t \u2192 0 (which may not hold in general), then the boundary term at infinity is 0, and the term at \u03b5 is (1 - Re \u03c6(\u03b5))/\u03b5. Therefore,\n\n\u222b_{\u03b5}^{\u221e} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 dt = (1 - Re \u03c6(\u03b5))/\u03b5 + \u222b_{\u03b5}^{\u221e} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt.\n\nSimilarly, for the integral from -\u221e to -\u03b5, since the integrand is even, it would be the same as from \u03b5 to \u221e. Hence, the integral over |t| \u2265 \u03b5 would be 2*( (1 - Re \u03c6(\u03b5))/\u03b5 + \u222b_{\u03b5}^{\u221e} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt ). But the original integral for E|X| is (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 dt = (1/\u03c0) \u222b_{R} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 dt (since integrand is even). Then, using integration by parts as above,\n\n(1/\u03c0) \u222b_{R} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 dt = (1/\u03c0) [ lim_{\u03b5 \u2192 0+} 2*( (1 - Re \u03c6(\u03b5))/\u03b5 + \u222b_{\u03b5}^{\u221e} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt ) ].\n\nBut as \u03b5 \u2192 0+, (1 - Re \u03c6(\u03b5))/\u03b5 \u2248 (1 - [1 - Var(X) \u03b5^2 / 2 + ... ])/\u03b5 \u2248 (Var(X) \u03b5^2 / 2)/\u03b5 = Var(X) \u03b5 / 2 \u2192 0. Therefore, the limit becomes (1/\u03c0) [ 0 + 2 \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt ] = (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt. However, Re \u03c6'(t) = - E[X sin(tX)], so this becomes - (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\u221e} E[X sin(tX)] / t dt. But since the integrand is odd in t when considering negative t, integrating over all R would involve \u222b_{-infty}^{infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/(\u03c0 t) dt = \u222b_{-infty}^{infty} [ - E[X sin(tX) ] / (\u03c0 t) ] dt = - (1/\u03c0) \u222b_{-infty}^{infty} E[X sin(tX) / t ] dt. But since X sin(tX)/t is even in t (since sin(-tX)/(-t) = sin(tX)/t), then \u222b_{-infty}^{infty} E[X sin(tX)/t] dt = 2 \u222b_{0}^{infty} E[X sin(tX)/t] dt. Therefore,\n\n- (1/\u03c0) \u222b_{-infty}^{infty} E[X sin(tX)/t ] dt = - (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{infty} E[X sin(tX)/t ] dt.\n\nBut from our earlier integration by parts result,\n\nE|X| = (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{infty} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 dt = (2/\u03c0) [ \u222b_{0}^{infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt ].\n\nBut \u222b_{0}^{infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt = \u222b_{0}^{infty} - E[X sin(tX)] / t dt = - E[ X \u222b_{0}^{infty} sin(tX)/t dt ].\n\nBut \u222b_{0}^{infty} sin(tX)/t dt = (\u03c0/2) sgn(X). Because \u222b_{0}^{infty} sin(at)/t dt = \u03c0/2 sgn(a). Hence, if X >0, \u222b_{0}^{infty} sin(tX)/t dt = \u03c0/2, and if X <0, it's -\u03c0/2. Therefore, \u222b_{0}^{infty} sin(tX)/t dt = (\u03c0/2) sgn(X). Hence,\n\nE[ X \u222b_{0}^{infty} sin(tX)/t dt ] = E[ X * (\u03c0/2) sgn(X) ] = (\u03c0/2) E[ |X| ].\n\nTherefore,\n\n(2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt = (2/\u03c0) * ( - (\u03c0/2) E|X| ) = - E|X|.\n\nWait, but we had E|X| = (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt, but according to this calculation, (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt = - E|X|. This seems contradictory. Wait, no:\n\nLet me retrace. From integration by parts:\n\nE|X| = (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2 dt = (2/\u03c0) [ lim_{\u03b5\u21920} (1 - Re \u03c6(\u03b5))/\u03b5 + \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt ].\n\nBut as \u03b5\u21920, (1 - Re \u03c6(\u03b5))/\u03b5 \u2248 (1 - (1 - Var(X) \u03b5^2 / 2 + ... )) / \u03b5 \u2248 (Var(X) \u03b5^2 / 2)/\u03b5 = Var(X) \u03b5 / 2 \u21920. So,\n\nE|X| = (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt.\n\nBut Re \u03c6'(t) = - E[X sin(tX)], so\n\n(2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt = - (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} E[X sin(tX)] / t dt = - (2/\u03c0) E[ X \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} sin(tX)/t dt ].\n\nAs above, \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} sin(tX)/t dt = (\u03c0/2) sgn(X), so,\n\n- (2/\u03c0) E[ X * (\u03c0/2) sgn(X) ] = - (2/\u03c0) * (\u03c0/2) E[ |X| ] = - E|X|.\n\nBut wait, this suggests that\n\nE|X| = - (2/\u03c0) \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt = - (1/\u03c0) \u222b_{-infty}^{\\infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt,\n\nsince the integrand is odd, so integrating from -infty to infty would be twice the integral from 0 to infty. Therefore,\n\nE|X| = - (1/\u03c0) \u222b_{-infty}^{\\infty} Re \u03c6'(t)/t dt.\n\nBut the problem states that E|X| = - (R) \u222b_{R} Re \u03c6'(t)/(\u03c0 t) dt, which matches this result. Therefore, this establishes part (b). However, the key steps involve integration by parts and using the fact that \u222b_{0}^{\\infty} sin(tX)/t dt = (\u03c0/2) sgn(X), which requires X to have finite expectation to apply Fubini's theorem and interchange the expectation and integral.\n\nTherefore, part (b) is proven by expressing E|X| using the integral of (1 - Re \u03c6(t))/t^2, performing integration by parts, and recognizing the resulting integral as involving Re \u03c6'(t)/t, leading to the final expression.\n\nIn summary, both parts (a) and (b) utilize integral representations of moments involving the characteristic function, with part (a) using a direct relationship for 0 < p < 2 and part (b) employing integration by parts for the case p=1 when the first absolute moment exists.", "deepseek_solution": "(a) To establish the equivalence, we utilize the integral representation of \\( |X|^p \\) for \\( 0 < p < 2 \\). By recognizing that:\n\n\\[\n|X|^p = C_p \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\cos(tX)}{|t|^{1 + p}} \\, dt,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( C_p = \\left[ \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\cos t}{|t|^{1 + p}} \\, dt \\right]^{-1} \\), we take expectations on both sides. Applying Fubini's theorem (justified by non-negativity) yields:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E}|X|^p = C_p \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{|t|^{1 + p}} \\, dt.\n\\]\n\nThe integral's finiteness directly corresponds to \\( \\mathrm{E}|X|^p < \\infty \\), proving the equivalence. The constant \\( C_p \\) evaluates to \\( \\frac{\\Gamma(1 + p)}{\\pi} \\sin \\frac{p \\pi}{2} \\) via known integrals involving the gamma function.\n\n(b) Assuming \\( \\mathrm{E}|X| < \\infty \\), express \\( |X| \\) as \\( \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\cos(tX)}{t^2} \\, dt \\). Taking expectations and integrating by parts:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E}|X| = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{t^2} \\, dt.\n\\]\n\nIntegration by parts transforms this into:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E}|X| = -\\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t)}{t} \\, dt,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t) = -\\mathrm{E}[X \\sin(tX)] \\). Recognizing \\( \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\sin(tX)}{t} \\, dt = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\operatorname{sgn}(X) \\) concludes the proof via Fubini's theorem.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n(a) \\(\\boxed{\\mathrm{E}|X|^p = \\frac{\\Gamma(1 + p)}{\\pi} \\sin \\frac{p \\pi}{2} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{|t|^{1 + p}} \\, dt}\\)\n\n(b) \\(\\boxed{\\mathrm{E}|X| = -\\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t)}{t} \\, dt}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n#### Part (a)\n1. Given $X$ is a random variable with characteristic function $\\varphi(t)$, our goal is to show $\\mathrm{E}|X|^p < \\infty$ for some $0 < p < 2$ if and only if\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{|t|^{1+p}} \\, dt < \\infty.\n\\]\n2. First, we recall the general result for $|X|^p$ (for $0 < p < 2$) which uses the Fubini's theorem and the integral representation:\n\\[\n|x|^p = C_p \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\cos(xt)}{|t|^{1+p}} \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere $C_p = \\left[ \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\cos t}{|t|^{1 + p}} \\, dt \\right]^{-1}$.\n   \n3. Since $\\varphi(t)$ is the characteristic function of $X$, we know:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t) = \\mathrm{E} [\\cos (tX)].\n\\]\n4. Substituting $\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)$, we get,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E} |X|^p = \\mathrm{E} \\left[ C_p \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\cos (tX)}{|t|^{1+p}} \\, dt \\right].\n\\]\n5. Using Fubini's theorem, we interchange the expectation and the integral:\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E} |X|^p = C_p \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\mathrm{E} [\\cos (tX)]}{|t|^{1+p}} \\, dt.\n\\]\n6. Therefore,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E} |X|^p = C_p \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{|t|^{1+p}} \\, dt.\n\\]\n7. For this expectation to be finite, it is necessary and sufficient that:\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{|t|^{1+p}} \\, dt < \\infty.\n\\]\n\nHence,\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{E} |X|^p = C_p \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1- \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{|t|^{1+p}} \\, dt}.\n\\]\n   \n#### Part (b)\n1. Given that $\\mathrm{E}|X| < \\infty$, we are tasked with showing:\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E}|X| = -(\\mathrm{R}) \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t)}{\\pi t} \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere $(\\mathrm{R}) \\int_{\\mathbb{R}}$ denotes the improper Riemann integral.\n\n2. Begin by differentiating the real part of the characteristic function $\\varphi(t)$:\n\\[\n[\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)]' = \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t),\n\\]\nsince $\\varphi'(t) = \\mathrm{E} [iX e^{i t X}] = i \\mathrm{E} [X \\cos(tX)] - \\mathrm{E} [X \\sin(tX)]$.\n\n3. Recognize that for a function $g$, the integration by parts for improper integrals gives:\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f'(t) g(t) \\, dt = \\left[ f(t) g(t) \\right]_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} - \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f(t) g'(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nassuming both integrals converge.\n\n4. Using $f(t) = 1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)$ and $g(t) = \\frac{1}{\\pi t}$, we apply integration by parts:\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E}|X| = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{\\pi t^2} \\, dt = \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty, b \\to \\infty, \\varepsilon \\to 0^+} \\left[ \\left(-\\frac{1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{\\pi t} \\right) \\Bigg|_a^{-\\varepsilon} + \\left(-\\frac{1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t)}{\\pi t}\\right) \\Bigg|_\\varepsilon^b - \\int_a^{- \\varepsilon} \\frac{\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t)}{\\pi t} \\, dt - \\int_\\varepsilon^b \\frac{\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t)}{\\pi t} \\, dt \\right].\n\\]\n\n5. Evaluating limits, noting $1 - \\operatorname{Re} \\varphi(t) = o(t)$ near $t=0$ and symmetrically as $t \\to \\pm \\infty$, yields:\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E}|X| = -\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty, b \\to \\infty, \\varepsilon \\to 0^+} \\left[ \\int_a^{-\\varepsilon} \\frac{\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t)}{\\pi t} \\, dt + \\int_\\varepsilon^b \\frac{\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t)}{\\pi t} \\, dt \\right].\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{E}|X| = - (\\mathrm{R}) \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t)}{\\pi t} \\, dt.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{E}|X| = - (\\mathrm{R}) \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} \\frac{\\operatorname{Re} \\varphi'(t)}{\\pi t} \\, dt}.\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Does there exist an integer whose cube, expressed in the decimal system, ends with the digit 1 repeated 2007 times?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out if there's an integer such that when you cube it, the result ends with 2007 consecutive 1s in the decimal system. Hmm, that's an interesting problem. Let me start breaking it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's understand what the question is asking. We need an integer n where n\u00b3 \u2261 111...111 (mod 10\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2077), right? The number 111...111 with 2007 ones is a number that ends with 2007 ones. So, essentially, n\u00b3 modulo 10\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2077 should be equal to this number. Since 10\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2077 is 2\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2077 multiplied by 5\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2077, maybe I can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem here. That theorem says that if we can solve the congruence modulo 2\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2077 and modulo 5\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2077 separately, then there's a solution modulo 10\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2077.\n\nSo, let's split the problem into two parts: solving n\u00b3 \u2261 111...111 mod 2\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2077 and n\u00b3 \u2261 111...111 mod 5\u00b2\u2070\u2070\u2070\u2077. If both of these have solutions, then by Chinese Re