tex \\( B \\) has more edges that have already been labeled, we ensure from previous steps that all edges connected to \\( B \\) retain \\( \\gcd = 1 \\).\n\n5. **Repeating the Process:**\n   - Identify any other vertex \\( C \\) in the graph which has an unlabeled edge.\n   - Repeat the path labeling process from \\( C \\), labeling newly encountered edges with consecutive numbers starting from the next available label.\n\n6. **Termination of the Procedure:**\n   - Continue the process until all edges of the graph \\( G \\) have been labeled.\n   - Since the graph is finite and connected, this process will eventually label all edges while maintaining the required gcd condition at each vertex.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nBy continuously creating paths and labeling edges consecutively, we ensure that every vertex with multiple connecting edges has at least two edges with labels that are consecutive, hence guaranteeing that their gcd is \\( 1 \\). Consequently, the desired labeling is always possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{We have shown that the labeling is possible such that at each vertex, the gcd of labels of edges is 1.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(ABCD\\) be a convex quadrilateral, and let \\(O\\) be the intersection point of the diagonals \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\). If \\(OA \\sin A + OC \\sin C = OB \\sin B + OD \\sin D\\), prove that \\(ABCD\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that a convex quadrilateral ABCD is cyclic, given that the sum OA\u00b7sin A + OC\u00b7sin C equals OB\u00b7sin B + OD\u00b7sin D, where O is the intersection of the diagonals AC and BD. Hmm, cyclic quadrilaterals... Right, a quadrilateral is cyclic if and only if the sum of its opposite angles is 180 degrees. Alternatively, there are some other criteria, like the power of a point, or maybe using the Law of Sines or Cosines in some triangles. \n\nFirst, let's recall som