cle is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + 0x + 0y -1 = 0 \u21d2 x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = 1.\n\nWait, that's interesting. The circumcircle of triangle ABC with coordinates A(0,1), B(-1,0), C(1,0) is the unit circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = 1.\n\nNow, check if P'( 2t/(t\u00b2 + 1 ), -(t\u00b2 - 1)/(t\u00b2 + 1 ) ) lies on the unit circle.\n\nCompute (2t/(t\u00b2 +1 ))\u00b2 + ( -(t\u00b2 -1)/(t\u00b2 +1 ) )\u00b2\n\n= (4t\u00b2)/(t\u00b2 +1 )\u00b2 + (t\u00b2 -1 )\u00b2/(t\u00b2 +1 )\u00b2\n\n= [4t\u00b2 + (t\u00b2 -1 )\u00b2 ] / (t\u00b2 +1 )\u00b2\n\nExpand (t\u00b2 -1 )\u00b2 = t^4 - 2t\u00b2 +1\n\nSo numerator becomes 4t\u00b2 + t^4 -2t\u00b2 +1 = t^4 +2t\u00b2 +1 = (t\u00b2 +1 )\u00b2\n\nTherefore, the expression simplifies to (t\u00b2 +1 )\u00b2 / (t\u00b2 +1 )\u00b2 =1\n\nTherefore, P' lies on the unit circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 =1, which is the circumcircle of triangle ABC. Therefore, the reflection P' lies on the circumcircle, as required.\n\nSo, even though the problem initially seemed contradictory due to the line being \"parallel to the two equal sides\", which in the original interpretation didn't make sense, by reinterpreting it as drawing a line through P such that segments PQ and PR are parallel to the equal sides AC and AB respectively (forming a parallelogram), the reflection of P over QR indeed lies on the circumcircle. This required careful analysis, setting up coordinates, and methodically computing the reflection and verifying its position on the circumcircle. The key insight was realizing the coordinates approach and meticulously working through the algebra to confirm the result.", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the problem, we start by placing the isosceles triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\) in a coordinate system with \\( B(-1, 0) \\), \\( C(1, 0) \\), and \\( A(0, 1) \\). Let \\( P(t, 0) \\) be an arbitrary point on \\( BC \\).\n\n1. **Constructing Points \\( Q \\) and \\( R \\):**\n   - Draw a line through \\( P \\) parallel to \\( AC \\) (slope \\( -1 \\)), intersecting